  "query_preview": "..\nA nearly-stationary col as well as upper-level divergence has\nmaintained the convectively active, wet pattern today. After \nmidnight, a lull in showers and light northerlies may allow for \nenough clearing for scattered, island-effect rainfall Monday \nafternoon. Due to the slow-moving nature of the convergence zone, \nisolated thunderstorms are possible through Monday. Then, drier \nconditions are possible for a few subsequent forecast periods as the\nactive weather pattern exits the region to the northwest. As for the\nlatter half of the week, northerlies may increase in speed to light \nto gentle. Shower coverage may differ as model guidance hints at a \npotential circulation developing to the east of the islands. Due to \nthe high uncertainty regarding this pattern, showers top out at 30 \npercent through Saturday with a slight chance of thunderstorms \nreturning. This will continue to be refined as confidence naturally \nincreases with time. Shortly thereafter, a dry spell is possible \nentering early next week.",
